  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nNot much has changed since the previous forecast cycle. High-\nresolution models continue to indicate a line of storms forming\nlate this evening just south of Lake Erie as an approaching \ntrough and associated cold front move into the region. \n\nThe most likely window remains between 7pm to 9pm across\nnorthwest PA and northeast OH. A few storms could be strong to \nsevere, particularly north and northwest of Pittsburgh, where a\ncombination of modest instability (900-1200J/kg of CAPE) and \neffective shear exceeding 30kts will support organized storm\nmodes (most likely in the form of bowing line segments). \nDamaging wind gusts will likely be the primary threat. However, \nany storms or notches in the line that are able to accelerate \nsoutheastward could acquire some low-level rotation based on\nmodel hodographs, potentially resulting in transient low-level\nmesocyclone features capable or producing a funnel cloud or \nbrief tornado.\n\nInstability is expected to begin waning as storms move toward \nthe Pittsburgh area between 9pm and 11pm, which could limit the\npotential for additional strong to severe storms. Lightning \nwould be the primary threat along with a marginal localized\nflash flood threat due to heavy rainfall.\n\nThe prolonged passage of the trough and lingering stationary\nboundary may support periods of moderate to heavy rainfall \ntotals during the day Thursday, potentially resulting in \nisolated flooding along creeks and streams as well as urban and\nlow-lying/poor-drainage areas. The greatest chance for this \noccurring is across northern WV, which will be in closer \nproximity to the slowed/stalled frontal boundary and therefore\nhas the greatest potential to see more persistent rounds of \nheavier showers and thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMachine-learning models suggest that after a brief period of \nridging on Friday, our large-scale weather pattern will \ntransition to a more zonal flow. As a result, this will allow \ndisturbances forming upstream (to our west) to track into our \narea. This active pattern could support heavy rain and/or \nsevere weather threats early next week and will need to be \nmonitored.",
